-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-* **Arbitrary Order:** If voxels are projected in arbitrary order, the final image is incorrect because the last projected voxel overwrites previous ones, regardless of depth or value [S4, unofficial]. Strict sorting or explicit max-comparison is required [S4, unofficial].
-* **Non-Axial Views/Holes:** For non-axial viewing angles, undersampling may cause holes in the projection, requiring morphological sampling and closing operations to reconstruct a continuous function [S1].
-* **Zero Voxels:** Zero-value voxels are excluded from processing as they do not contribute to the maximum in typical positive-valued volumes, providing significant compression [S1].
+**Parameters:**
+*   **Threshold for "Interesting" Voxels:** 0. Voxels with value $\le 0$ are excluded [S1].
+*   **Sorting Order:** Ascending (low to high) [S1, S3].
+
+**Pitfalls:**
+*   **Arbitrary Order:** If voxels are projected in arbitrary order, the final image is incorrect because the last projected voxel overwrites previous ones, regardless of depth or value [S4, unofficial].
+*   **Undersampling Holes:** Non-axial views may exhibit holes due to sparse sampling of the volume by the projection rays [S1].
+*   **Precision:** Overwriting assumes that the value-sorted order perfectly aligns with the MIP requirement. If the volume contains negative values and the background is not handled correctly, the initialization of the image buffer must be to $-\infty$ or the minimum possible value [G].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
-### Object-order Binary Volume Projection
-An early object-order method for binary volumes where voxels with value one are projected onto the screen in back-to-front order [S5, unofficial]. The data plane is traversed plane-by-plane and row-by-row inside each plane to ensure correct visibility [S5, unofficial]. This ordered traversal is implemented with three nested loops indexing the $x$-, $y$-, and $z$-directions respectively [S5, unofficial]. For arbitrary orientations, axes are traversed in increasing or decreasing order as needed [S5, unofficial]. Axis-parallel clipping planes limit traversal to a smaller rectangular region by modifying loop bounds [S5, unofficial]. A depth image is generated by overwriting pixel values with the distance of the projected voxel from the image plane [S5, unofficial].
+### [Object-order Binary Volume Projection](6605-object-order-binary-volume-projection.md)
+Handles binary data (0/1) using back-to-front traversal for occlusion, without a Z-buffer.
+
+### [Front-to-Back Object-Order Projection](11222-front-to-back-object-order-projection.md)
+Supports continuous values and transparency, using front-to-back traversal for early ray termination.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-* **Time Complexity:** $O(N \log N)$ for sorting $N$ interesting voxels, plus $O(N)$ for projection [G]. The sorting step is dominant if $N$ is large, but $N$ is often much smaller than the total voxel count due to zero-voxel removal [S1].
-* **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ to store the sorted array of interesting voxels and the image buffer [G].
-* **Performance:** Substantial reduction in processed voxels (sometimes more than 95%) is achieved by filtering zeros [S1].
-* **Strengths:** Efficient for MIP rendering on GPUs by avoiding read-modify-write cycles using simple overwrites due to sorting [S1, S3]. Enables compression-domain rendering; MIP can be computed directly on the value-sorted array without full 3D reconstruction [S1].
-* **Limitations:** Requires pre-sorting, which adds overhead [G]. Not naturally suited for alpha-blending without additional complexity, as the simple overwrite strategy relies on the max operation [S1, S3]. Susceptible to aliasing/holes in non-axial views without morphological post-processing [S1].
-* **When to use:** Ideal for Maximum Intensity Projection (MIP) of sparse or large volumetric datasets where transparency is not required [S1, S3].
-* **When not to use:** When alpha-blending is needed, or when the volume is dense (few zeros to filter), making sorting overhead unjustified [S1, S3].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Dominated by the sorting step, $O(N \log N)$ or $O(N)$ with histogram-based sorting, where $N$ is the number of non-zero voxels [S1, S3]. The projection step is $O(N)$ [S3].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for storing the value-sorted array and cumulative histogram [S1, S3].
+*   **Performance:** Substantial reduction in processed voxels (often >95%) by excluding zero voxels [S1]. Allows compression-domain rendering as MIP can be computed directly on the value-sorted array without full 3D reconstruction [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Efficient for MIP; avoids Z-buffer contention; suitable for sparse volumes [S1, S3].
+*   **Limitations:** Primarily suited for MIP or simple overwriting compositing; not directly applicable to alpha-blending without modification; requires sorting overhead [S1, S3].
+*   **When to Use:** When computing Maximum Intensity Projections on sparse volumetric data where performance is critical and transparency is not required [S1, S3].
+*   **When Not to Use:** When alpha-blending or transparency is needed, or when the volume is dense (sorting overhead may not be justified) [S1, S3].
 
 ## Background
-* **Origin:** The object-order approach is a fundamental technique in volume rendering, historically contrasted with image-order (ray-casting) methods [S2, unofficial]. The specific application to MIP with value-sorted arrays for GPU acceleration is detailed in works on multiresolution MIP rendering [S1, S3].
-* **History:** Early object-order algorithms, such as those by Upson and Keeler, processed voxels in front-to-back order for binary volumes [S4, unofficial]. The use of value-sorted arrays for MIP leverages the mathematical property that the maximum intensity is determined by the highest value, allowing a simple overwrite if processed in ascending order [S1, S3].
-* **Usage:** Commonly used in medical imaging for MIP visualization of CT scans, where bone and contrast agents have high values and background is zero [S1][S2, unofficial].
+The method was introduced as an object-order voxel-projection technique [MKG00] to avoid processing empty space in volume rendering [S3]. It was notably applied in the context of morphological pyramids for multiresolution MIP rendering, where it allowed for efficient progressive refinement by storing data in value-sorted arrays [S1]. The approach is part of the broader object-space rendering paradigm, which projects contributions from the volume to the image plane, contrasting with image-space ray tracing [S2, S4, unofficial].
 
